{
  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The artillery attacks on Knin, Benkovac, Obrovac and Gračac constitute extensive destruction not justified by military necessity, therefore they constitute a war crime under Article 8(2)(b)(iv) of the Rome Statute.",
      "reasoning": "Article 8(2)(b)(iv) criminalises \"Extensive destruction and appropriation of property, not justified by military necessity and carried out unlawfully and wantonly\". The Trial Chamber found that the HV could not identify legitimate military targets in the four towns and applied a 200‑metre margin of error, treating impacts beyond that distance as unlawful. The large‑scale artillery bombardment caused widespread damage to civilian infrastructure and forced the mass departure of civilians, evidencing destruction that was not proportionate to any concrete military advantage and thus not justified by military necessity.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(b)(iv)",
          "quoted_text": "Extensive destruction and appropriation of property, not justified by military necessity and carried out unlawfully and wantonly"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The attacks were directed against civilian objects, i.e., towns and dwellings that were not military objectives, violating Article 8(2)(b)(ii) of the Rome Statute.",
      "reasoning": "Article 8(2)(b)(ii) prohibits \"Intentionally directing attacks against civilian objects, that is, objects which are not military objectives\". The Chamber’s analysis showed that the artillery impacts were located in residential areas of the four towns, beyond any identifiable military target, and that the HV could not locate targets of opportunity such as moving police or military vehicles. Consequently, the attacks were directed at civilian objects rather than legitimate military objectives.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(b)(ii)",
          "quoted_text": "Intentionally directing attacks against civilian objects, that is, objects which are not military objectives"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The conduct of the artillery attacks violated the principle of distinction and proportionality, customary rules of international humanitarian law, because the HV failed to take feasible precautions to verify targets and to limit incidental civilian harm.",
      "reasoning": "Customary IHL requires parties to distinguish between civilians and combatants and to ensure that attacks are not excessive in relation to the anticipated military advantage. The evidence that the HV could not identify targets of opportunity, relied on a blanket 200‑metre error margin, and ignored expert testimony on wind and temperature effects demonstrates a failure to take feasible precautions. This breach of distinction and proportionality renders the attacks unlawful, even if some military advantage was claimed.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "Even assuming the presence of some military objectives, the indiscriminate nature of the artillery fire—evidenced by the inability to differentiate targets and the massive civilian exodus—makes the attacks unlawful under the customary rule prohibiting indiscriminate attacks.",
      "reasoning": "Customary rule 14 (distinction) obliges parties to direct operations only against military objectives. The Chamber’s finding that the HV could not identify moving police or military vehicles, combined with the large number of civilians fleeing the area, shows that the attacks were not directed at specific military objectives and were therefore indiscriminate. Indiscriminate attacks are prohibited irrespective of any claimed military necessity.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
}
